= Implicature — Technical =

== Gricean derivation ==
A simplified conversational derivation uses:

<pre>
literal/conventional content
+ conversational expectations
+ contextual assumptions
+ available alternatives
→ inferred speaker meaning
</pre>

The inference is defeasible rather than deductively entailed.

== Scalar implicature ==
For a scale such as:

<pre>
some < all
</pre>

an utterance with the weaker item can implicate the negation of the stronger alternative when the stronger statement would have been relevant and appropriate.

Modern accounts differ over whether scalar enrichment is generated primarily by pragmatic reasoning, grammar, probabilistic social inference, or hybrid mechanisms.

== Generalized and particularized implicature ==
Some implicatures arise reliably without unusually rich context; others depend heavily on a specific conversational situation.

The distinction is useful but not a claim that context is ever absent.

== Relevance and probabilistic approaches ==
Relevance theory emphasizes cognitive relevance and inferential enrichment. Rational/pragmatic Bayesian models formalize listeners as reasoning about speaker choices under uncertainty.

These models can reproduce graded interpretations that classical categorical accounts idealize.

== Epistemic caution ==
Observed listener inferences do not by themselves establish speaker intention, and corpus co-occurrence does not directly identify implicature.

Experimental designs must distinguish lexical meaning, world knowledge, task effects, and pragmatic inference.

